  [1mThe design you approved:[0m
  - [38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m keeps its signature. Instead of taking a userId, it returns one: [38;5;153m{ success, user, userId [39m
    [38;5;153m}[39m. A userId supplied by the client before login wouldn't be verified, so the ID should come back from the login
    itself. For now the stub uses the username as the ID.
  - A new [38;5;153msession.js[39m module stores the current user in localStorage as [38;5;153m{ userId, username, loggedInAt }[39m. It has three
    functions: [38;5;153msetCurrentUser[39m, [38;5;153mgetCurrentUser[39m and [38;5;153mclearCurrentUser[39m. Other forms will import it to find out who's logged
    in.
  - The login page switches to ES modules ([38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m), so pages need to be served over HTTP rather than
    opened from disk.
  - A failed login leaves the current user logged in. Bad stored data reads as "not logged in". If storage isn't
    available, the user isn't saved, but nothing crashes.
  - I'll set up unit tests with Node's built-in test runner ([38;5;153mnpm test[39m), with nine planned tests for [38;5;153msession.js[39m. Changes
    to [38;5;153mapp.js[39m get checked by hand in the browser.
  - Not included: logout UI, login history, a server-side session, and lint or end-to-end tests.

  [1mTwo gaps I filled myself while checking the spec:[0m
  - Loading [38;5;153msession.js[39m under Node, where [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m doesn't exist, must not throw. I added a tenth test for this.
  - [38;5;153mclearCurrentUser[39m handles storage errors the same way [38;5;153msetCurrentUser[39m does.

